  "protocol_version": "1.0.0-rc.13",
  "rule": "protocol/environments.md section 8.4.1: a failed read, stat, or parse of any state or surface file is never reported as absence. Each case pins one file class (marker, lock, provisioning seed, credential passthrough entry, backup-record inventory) to its typed, present-or-absent discriminating inputs and the expected diagnostic. Failure classes: permission-denied (EACCES-class), io-error (EIO-class), unparseable-content (bytes fail decoding or parsing), schema-invalid-content (parsed content fails schema validation for a reason other than version), symlink-where-regular-required, directory-where-file-expected, parent-not-directory (ENOTDIR-class: the entry exists but a path component above it is not a directory, so the open fails). presence/entry_kind are ground truth: permission-denied and io-error strike at the open/read stage after lstat. Boundary verification precedes readability classification (sections 1.3, 4, 8.2, 10.1): a marker failure that violates the section 4 contract, or that prevents proving the boundary (symlink, directory, or unstatable marker path), is environment_store_untrusted and is outside this family; the marker cases therefore cover the open/read/parse stages only. Seed unparseable-content applies where the seed path parses (the codex_cli revision-B mcp_servers strip, which MUST parse as TOML); elsewhere seeds are unparsed byte copies. Passthrough entries carry no content and require a link, so unparseable-content, schema-invalid-content, and symlink-where-regular-required do not apply to them; a directory at the entry path is 'no longer a symlink' and stays detached-side. currency 'unknown' pins the section 8.4.1 rule that an unreadable file makes the affected row non-current with currency unknown; absence-side outcomes carry currency 'known'. repair_relinks pins that --repair re-links a detached entry and leaves an unreadable entry untouched. repair is env resolve --repair and update is profile update (sections 10.1, 9.2): against an unreadable lock both refuse with environment_store_untrusted and rebuild nothing (rebuilt) and write nothing (written), repair emitting no fragment; restore restores the newest backup generation (env unmanage --restore-backups, section 9.2) and stops before mutating (written) when the generations inventory cannot be established. The backup-record class covers the generations inventory: an unreadable inventory is environment_backup_record_unreadable with currency unknown, while a known-empty inventory is current with currency known.",
  "schema_version": 1,
